0354: SweetFramebuffer, SweetSurface, SweetHID: scanout pool for the window server's whole ring, one way of presenting while a ring surface cannot be scanned out, panel window cropped by the source address in both paths

Needs 0350, 0352 and 0353. With kern.sweet_fb_scanout=0 what changes is the window (item 4), 2x frames of
several layers (item 3) and that a copied frame's SwapEnd is not held (item 6).

Seen on the phone with 0353 and kern.sweet_fb_scanout=1 after hours of use with restarts of backboardd: the
whole screen jumped a few millimetres up and to the left, repeatedly; "pool: 4 of 4 slots in use, 31
surfaces made in it, 14 refused (full)"; in 10 s "copied instead: not from the pool" +52 and ~5 plane mode
changes a second. Two causes: the pool runs out (CoreAnimation keeps three screen surfaces, makes three new
ones when a page's needs change, and a restarted backboardd makes its own while the kernel holds the one on
the panel), and the copy (blit_scale(), origin through GRAS_A2D_SRC_XMIN/YMIN) and the scanout (the plane's
SRC_XY) do not put the window in the same place unless both engines apply their origin.

1. The pool has eight slots of 5 MiB (SWEET_FB_SCAN_SLOTS; 0xe5000000-0xe7800000, below the QSEE pool).
2. A swapped surface that cannot be scanned out is noted (fScanBlock[]: id and pointer, not retained); while
   one of them is alive every frame goes the copy's way, so the plane changes mode when the ring changes,
   not frame by frame.
3. A 2x frame of several layers is shown by its layer 0 alone when that is the window server's full-screen
   surface, otherwise not at all; present() no longer flips the back buffer for a 2x frame it did not copy.
4. The window: fOffX is the multiple of 24 nearest the centre (24: the window at 16, 12 of the 2x picture),
   and SweetHID's touch mapping uses the same rule. The origin starts on 64 bytes, so both paths crop by the
   source address: the plane's address (rows and 16-byte groups of columns; SRC_XY 0, 0) and the blit's
   texture base (rows and 16-pixel groups; the rest in GRAS_A2D_SRC_XMIN/YMIN).
5. kern.sweet_fb_scanout_diag: a line per slot (surface, IOSurfaceName, creator, age, swaps), the surfaces
   of item 2, frames shown by layer 0, and where the window's origin goes. SweetSurface keeps who made a
   surface, when, and how often it was swapped.
6. The scanout needs kern.sweet_fb_swapwait 1 or 2 (setting 0 while scanning stops it); the SwapEnd after a
   wait for every swap is held only if a scanned-out frame may be on the panel, up to 250 ms instead of 50
   (on the phone with 0353: 78 holds, 14 at the limit).

Checked on the host: CoreAnimation's ring with display on/off, backboardd restarts and transient
screen-sized surfaces (30 seeds x 30 s): with eight slots every frame scanned out, at most five slots used,
no plane mode change in steady state; and blit_scale() and the window computation extracted with a model of
both engines: the two paths agree to 1.27 panel px whichever engine ignores its origin.
Measured on the phone (one boot): with kern.sweet_fb_scanout=1 set before backboardd starts, 1140 frames,
1140 flips, pool 4 of 8, 0 refused; set at run time, 0 frames scan out (the existing surfaces are not in the
pool). The framing has not been checked by eye.
On: kern.sweet_fb_swapwait=1 (default) and kern.sweet_fb_scanout=1 with the window server's surfaces in the
pool (kern.sweet_fb_scanout=2 and a restart of backboardd). Off: kern.sweet_fb_scanout=2 or 0.

